= Resource Description Framework =

== Overview ==
The '''Resource Description Framework''' ('''RDF''') is a W3C framework and data model for representing information as graphs of statements.

An RDF statement is usually expressed as a triple:

<pre>
subject — predicate — object
</pre>

Example:

<pre>
Mars — orbits — Sun
</pre>

Collections of triples form RDF graphs.

RDF is a foundation of the [[Semantic Web]] and is widely used for linked data, metadata and [[Knowledge graph|knowledge graphs]].

== RDF terms ==
RDF uses several kinds of terms.

'''IRI''' — globally scoped identifier for resources and properties.

'''Literal''' — value such as a string, number or date.

'''Blank node''' — locally scoped node without a global IRI.

RDF 1.2 additionally defines triple terms for referring to triples within RDF data.

== Subject, predicate, object ==
In a triple:

<pre>
s p o
</pre>

the subject identifies/describes a resource.

The predicate is an RDF [[Property|property]].

The object is another resource, blank node, literal or, in RDF 1.2, permitted triple-term form under the specification.

A graph is a set of such triples.

== RDF graph ==
An RDF graph is not ordered.

Two serializations containing the same triples represent the same abstract graph even if line order differs.

Graph semantics depends on identifiers and blank-node structure, not on presentation order.

== RDF dataset ==
An RDF dataset contains:

* one default graph;
* zero or more named graphs.

Named graphs associate graph content with graph names.

Applications use datasets for source separation, provenance, access control and other purposes, but the meaning of graph names depends on application/specification conventions.

== Serialization ==
RDF is a data model, not one file syntax.

Common serializations include:
* Turtle;
* N-Triples;
* TriG;
* JSON-LD;
* RDF/XML.

The same graph can be serialized in several formats.

Therefore:

<pre>
RDF ≠ Turtle
RDF ≠ RDF/XML
RDF ≠ JSON-LD
</pre>

== RDF semantics ==
RDF has a formal [[Semantics|semantics]].

An interpretation assigns denotations to RDF terms and extensions to properties.

A graph G '''entails''' graph E when every relevant interpretation satisfying G also satisfies E.

Different entailment regimes add different vocabulary semantics.

== RDF Schema ==
RDF Schema (RDFS) adds vocabulary for:

* classes;
* properties;
* subclass;
* subproperty;
* domain;
* range.

These have inferential meaning.

For example:

<pre>
Dog rdfs:subClassOf Mammal
Fido rdf:type Dog
</pre>

can entail:

<pre>
Fido rdf:type Mammal
</pre>

under RDFS semantics.

== RDF and OWL ==
[[Web Ontology Language|OWL]] builds on the Semantic Web/RDF ecosystem to express richer ontology axioms.

RDF itself does not supply all OWL semantics.

An RDF graph can contain OWL vocabulary, but reasoning requires the corresponding OWL semantics/entailment regime.

== Open-world behavior ==
RDF/OWL knowledge systems are commonly interpreted under an open-world assumption.

If the graph does not contain:

<pre>
Alice hasEmail ...
</pre>

that does not entail that Alice has no email.

Missing information remains unknown unless explicit semantics say otherwise.

== Blank nodes ==
Blank nodes identify graph-local existential resources.

A blank node should not automatically be interpreted as:
* a secret entity;
* an entity lacking any real identifier;
* a stable identifier across datasets.

Its formal role is local within RDF graph/dataset semantics.

== RDF and validation ==
RDF syntax/semantics does not by itself enforce every application constraint.

For example, a data model may require every Person to have exactly one birth date.

Constraint languages such as SHACL are commonly used to validate such requirements.

This differs from ontology inference.

== RDF 1.2 ==
As of 2026, W3C RDF 1.2 Concepts and RDF 1.2 Semantics are Candidate Recommendation Snapshots.

RDF 1.2 extends RDF 1.1 with features including:
* triple terms;
* directional language-tagged strings;
* version-signaling mechanisms described by the specification suite.

The stable RDF graph/dataset model remains recognizable across RDF 1.1 and 1.2.

Because RDF 1.2 is still in the Candidate Recommendation process, version-specific deployment claims should state the implementation/specification version.

== RDF and knowledge graphs ==
RDF is commonly used for [[Knowledge graph|knowledge graphs]] because:
* identifiers are globally scoped;
* triples form a graph;
* vocabularies can be shared;
* formal semantics support inference;
* SPARQL can query graph patterns.

But not every RDF graph is usefully called a knowledge graph, and knowledge graphs can use other graph models.

== Epistemic notes ==
An RDF triple is a represented assertion. Its presence in a graph does not make the assertion true. Truth, evidence and provenance remain separate from syntactic/semantic well-formedness.

